UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 10, Lesson 3: Multiplication word problems', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Multiplication word problems describe equal groups or arrays; the factors match groups×size (or rows×columns).","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite a multiplication and solve: \"Three shelves hold 8 books each. How many books?\"\n\nFEEDBACK\n3×8=24 books.\n\nWATCH FOR\nAdding the numbers in the story without equal groups (3+8) answers a different question.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nSolve eight multiplication word problems (groups and arrays). For each, write the expression, a quick sketch and a sentence answer. Invent two new problems for a partner.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Multiplication word problems describe equal groups or arrays; the factors match groups×size (or rows×columns).","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Problem solving","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-MR-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Solve eight multiplication word problems (groups and arrays). For each, write the expression, a quick sketch and a sentence answer. Invent two new problems for a partner. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: 3×8=24 books. Reasoning: I used word problem by attending to equal groups and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Adding the numbers in the story without equal groups (3+8) answers a different question. Correction: 3×8=24 books. Why: the correction restores accurate use of word problem.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Adding the numbers in the story without equal groups (3+8) answers a different question. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses word problem with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with word problem, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values. / Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"3×8=24 books. This matches the lesson skill for word problem.","options":["3×8=24 books.","Adding the numbers in the story without equal groups (3+8) answers a different question.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Write a multiplication and solve: \"Three shelves hold 8 books each. How many books?\" Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Adding the numbers in the story without equal groups (3+8) answers a different question. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of equal groups and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Adding the numbers in the story without equal groups (3+8) answers a different question.","Multiplication word problems describe equal groups or arrays; the factors match groups×size (or rows×columns).","3×8=24 books."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising word problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named equal groups and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the equal groups and explain why it matters used."],"question":"A learner is practising word problem. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value or quantity.","options":["Write only a final number with no working.","Change digits to look neater without checking place value.","Adding the numbers in the story without equal groups (3+8) answers a different question.","Show the places, values or steps and say why each step is valid."],"question":"Which working note best supports a mathematics answer about word problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the method; the others break it.","options":["Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","4 bags with 6 apples each → 4×6=24 apples. An array of 5 rows of 3 seats → 5×3=15 seats.","Adding the numbers in the story without equal groups (3+8) answers a different question."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Multiplication word problems describe equal groups or arrays; the factors match groups×size (or rows×columns). — which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus.","options":["Delete units so the answers appear to match.","Average the two answers without checking either.","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed.","Pick the longer-looking working automatically."],"question":"Which error should you repair first if two methods disagree?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same method.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Solve a fresh problem with different numbers and explain the method.","Retype the worked model numbers as the answer.","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers."],"question":"Which practice choice shows transfer of word problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Useful feedback names the failed step and shows corrected working.","options":["Tell the learner to skip working and only keep the final digits.","Just add more digits until it looks longer.","Name the exact place or step that failed, then show corrected working for word problem.","Praise neat handwriting even when the places are wrong."],"question":"Which feedback comment is most useful after a mistake?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses word problem with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: 3×8=24 books. Reasoning: I used word problem by attending to equal groups and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with word problem, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Adding the numbers in the story without equal groups (3+8) answers a different question. Correction: 3×8=24 books. Why: the correction restores accurate use of word problem.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y3-w10-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise word problem.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Number and Algebra","success_criteria":["I can use word problem with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify equal groups and explain why it matters.","I can practise array independently on a new prompt.","I can notice product needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Multiplication word problems describe equal groups or arrays; the factors match groups×size (or rows×columns). Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Multiplication word problems describe equal groups or arrays; the factors match groups×size (or rows×columns)."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about word problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n4 bags with 6 apples each → 4×6=24 apples. An array of 5 rows of 3 seats → 5×3=15 seats.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite a multiplication and solve: \"Three shelves hold 8 books each. How many books?\"\n\nFEEDBACK\n3×8=24 books.","explain":"Multiplication word problems describe equal groups or arrays; the factors match groups×size (or rows×columns).\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Adding the numbers in the story without equal groups (3+8) answers a different question. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Equal-groups story matched to multiplication.","caption":"Factors match groups and group size.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Groups</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">4 bags of 6</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Product</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">24</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: 4 bags with 6 apples each → 4×6=24 apples. An array of 5 rows of 3 seats → 5×3=15 seats. Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: 4 bags with 6 apples each → 4×6=24 apples. An array of 5 rows of 3 seats → 5×3=15 seats. Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about word problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n4 bags with 6 apples each → 4×6=24 apples. An array of 5 rows of 3 seats → 5×3=15 seats. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Array story contrasted with a wrong addition.","caption":"Structure decides the expression.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Array</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">5 rows of 3</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Not</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">3+8 for shelves of 8</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about word problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAdding the numbers in the story without equal groups (3+8) answers a different question. Here is a more productive model: 4 bags with 6 apples each → 4×6=24 apples. An array of 5 rows of 3 seats → 5×3=15 seats.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\n3×8=24 books.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits 